# Detecting Credit Card Fraud with Machine Learning

This notebook builds and compares several approaches for detecting fraudulent transactions in the Kaggle **Credit Card Fraud Detection** dataset (284,807 transactions, of which only ~0.17% are fraud). Because the classes are so imbalanced, accuracy is misleading, so models are judged on **recall for the fraud class, confusion matrices, and ROC AUC**.

## Approach

1. **Exploratory analysis with balanced sampling.** Fraud cases are paired with an equal-sized random sample of legitimate transactions. Features are standardized, and variables whose means differ most between fraud and non-fraud are selected. Calculating pair plots for all 30 features is expensive, so pair plots are drawn only for these features. They show the two classes are largely separable.
2. **Distance-based classification.** After undersampling the training set (1:5 fraud to non-fraud) and applying `RobustScaler`, each transaction is assigned to the class whose mean vector it is closest to, using three distance metrics:
   - Mahalanobis (uses the inverse covariance matrix of each class)
   - Euclidean
   - Manhattan
3. **Soft-voting ensemble.** An ensemble of L1-regularized Logistic Regression, Random Forest, and XGBoost is trained on an undersampled, robust-scaled training set. Predictions are made by averaging the predicted probabilities.

## Results (held-out 20% test set, 98 fraud cases)

| Model | Fraud caught | False alarms | ROC AUC |
|---|---|---|---|
| Mahalanobis distance | 95 / 98 | 6,890 | 0.924 |
| Euclidean distance | 77 / 98 | 17 | 0.893 |
| Manhattan distance | 79 / 98 | 19 | 0.903 |
| **Soft-voting ensemble** | **89 / 98** | **469** | **0.988** |

The ensemble gives the best overall ranking ability (ROC AUC 0.988) with 91% fraud recall. Mahalanobis distance catches the most fraud but produces many false positives. Euclidean and Manhattan are highly precise but miss about 20% of fraud cases.

## Tools
Python, pandas, NumPy, scikit-learn, imbalanced-learn, XGBoost, SciPy, Matplotlib, Seaborn
